 ## Background
-The method was introduced by Jung, Kong, and Kim in 2022/2024 [S1, unofficial][S2]. It builds on Case-Based Reasoning (CBR) principles in volume rendering, addressing the difficulty of manual TF design. It improves upon Kohlmann et al.'s approach [KBKG09] which used only intensity profile matching with ED for linking 2D slice positions for a single SOI, by retrieving multiple SOIs for full TF design using a two-stage CBR scheme [S2]. The method was evaluated on the 3D-IRCADb-01 dataset [SHA*10] using the Voreen volume rendering engine [MSRMH09] on an Intel i7 CPU and Nvidia GTX 980 Ti GPU [S1, unofficial].
+The method was introduced by Jung, Kong, and Kim in 2022/2024 [S1, unofficial][S2]. It builds on Case-Based Reasoning (CBR) principles in volume rendering, addressing the difficulty of manual TF design [S1, unofficial][S2]. It improves upon Kohlmann et al.'s approach [KBKG09] which used only intensity profile matching with ED for linking 2D slice positions for a single SOI, by retrieving multiple SOIs for full TF design using a two-stage CBR scheme [S2]. The method was evaluated on the 3D-IRCADb-01 dataset [SHA*10] using the Voreen volume rendering engine [MSRMH09] on an Intel i7 CPU and Nvidia GTX 980 Ti GPU [S1, unofficial].
 
